1. [MAJOR] tests/agent_permissions_test.rs:191 — `permission_action` treats **any** all-deny pattern object as equivalent to a bare `"deny"`, so a *narrow* deny passes every check in the suite while leaving the key allowed for everything the pattern does not match — `permission_action` (191-224) returns the single action when every pattern agrees (209-218) and never asks whether the pattern covers everything; `check_reviewer_denies` (326-348) and `check_no_grants_beyond` (292-316) therefore accept `agent.reviewer.permission.external_directory = {"*.md": "deny"}` as "deny", and `project_config_pins_reviewer_read_only_and_verifier_privileged` (523-544) only inspects `edit`/`bash` via `Value::as_str`, so nothing else objects. This is not a harmless normalisation: the phase's own measured ruleset (phases/phase-084/REPORT.md:911-912, "the last matching rule wins") shows the global config's `external_directory ** allow` at REPORT.md:928 sitting *above* the agent's rule, so a deny that only matches `*.md` leaves every other path governed by that global allow — the reviewer could read outside the checkout while the suite stays green. The suite's own object-form test (946-989) only exercises `{"**": "deny"}`, so there is no test on the failing side, and the suite's semantics are inverted: it rejects a narrow *allow* (`{"**":"deny","/tmp/*":"allow"}`, 971-978) but accepts a narrow *deny*, which is the dangerous direction. The doc comment at 184-190 ("The object form is the same rule as the bare one when every pattern in it carries the same action") states the false premise the code implements. Concrete fix: `permission_action` must only treat an object as equivalent to a bare deny when its pattern is a catch-all (`*`/`**`), or `check_reviewer_denies` must require the bare action for the eleven denied keys; add an `edge_*` case for a narrow-pattern deny (`{"*.md":"deny"}`) that must be rejected. VERIFIED: traced the exact path by hand — `permission_action` → `check_reviewer_denies` → `check_no_grants_beyond` — and walked every test in `tests/agent_permissions_test.rs` against the mutated file; none fails. (Note the dispatcher's `assert_reviewer_read_only` reads the same two shapes, so it inherits the same hole.)
